<h2>Defence and counterclaim are different moves</h2>
<p>Section 23A actually authorises two things, and conflating them loses value. The first is defensive: the respondent may oppose the petitioner's relief on the ground of the petitioner's own adultery, cruelty or desertion — the classic answer to a restitution petition, where the withdrawal from society is justified by the petitioner's conduct. The second is offensive: the respondent may seek a decree. A respondent who only pleads the petitioner's cruelty as a defence gets, at best, dismissal of the petition — and remains married. A respondent who counterclaims converts the same facts into a route out of the marriage. In a litigation where both spouses have in substance given up on the relationship but each wants the decree on their own terms, the counterclaim is what keeps the respondent from leaving court empty-handed.</p>

<h2>How it is filed and tried</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Plead it in the written statement.</strong> The counterclaim travels with the written statement, set out with the same particularity as a petition — specific acts, dates and places of the cruelty, adultery or desertion alleged.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Pay the court fee.</strong> The counterclaim is in substance a cross-petition and carries its own court fee, as a petition under the Act would.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. The petitioner answers.</strong> The petitioner files a written statement to the counterclaim; issues are framed covering both cases.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. One trial, two verdicts.</strong> Evidence is recorded once. The court can dismiss the petition and decree the counterclaim, decree both in part, or dismiss both — each outcome appealable under Section 28.</div>
</div>

<h2>Choosing between counterclaim and separate petition</h2>
<p>The counterclaim wins on speed and economy, but three situations still favour a separate petition. Where the respondent's ground is outside the Section 23A trio — mental disorder, conversion, renunciation — the section simply does not reach it. Where territorial convenience matters — the petition is in a distant court and the respondent could maintain her own petition where she resides — a separate petition plus a transfer application may serve better than a counterclaim in the inconvenient forum. And where the respondent's case needs time to assemble while the petition is being contested, the counterclaim's timing inside the written statement can force premature commitment; a later substantive petition keeps the option open, at the price of a second proceeding.</p>

<div class="check">
  <p>Drafting pointers: do not plead the counterclaim as a narrative of grievance — plead it as a petition, with each statutory ingredient matched to facts; claim interim reliefs in the counterclaim too, since Section 24 maintenance and expenses run in favour of whichever spouse qualifies; and remember the statutory bars of Section 23 (condonation, collusion, delay) apply to the counterclaim exactly as to a petition, because the court decrees relief on the counterclaim only if satisfied the claimant is not taking advantage of their own wrong.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this explainer is for general information only and is not legal advice. Parallel provisions exist in other matrimonial statutes; this article covers the Hindu Marriage Act.</p>
